    "myth": "Lucifer led a rebellion in heaven and a third of the angels fell with him.",
    "truth": "The only text behind this idea is Revelation 12:4, where a dragon's tail sweeps a third of the stars to earth. Revelation is highly symbolic, and building a doctrine of fallen angels from one image takes a lot of exegetical gymnastics.",
    "detail": "Revelation itself says that stars stand for the “angels” (messengers) of the seven churches (Rev 1:20), and the word translated angel can mean a human messenger. In the Old Testament God likened the stars to Abraham's descendants. The book notes that some scholars read the verse as a third of Israel destroyed by Rome in the war of AD 67–70, alongside many other proposed meanings. The rebellion story has more in common with Jewish mysticism and the book of Enoch, which names Azazel, not Lucifer, as the rebel. Sound practice is to establish doctrine by two or three witnesses. Nobody knows exactly where the devil and demons came from, so the idea can be held as possible but should not be taught as doctrine.",
    "scripture": "Revelation 12:4; Revelation 1:20; Genesis 15:5; Deuteronomy 19:15",
    "note": "Many traditional interpreters do read Revelation 12:4 alongside 12:7–9, where the dragon's angels are cast down, as describing an angelic fall. Others take it as purely symbolic, so the passage remains contested."
