    "detail": "In the NASB the word “hell” appears about 15 times in the whole Bible, 11 of them in the Gospels. The Hebrew and Greek behind it are Sheol and Hades (the grave or pit) and Gehenna (the Valley of Hinnom, a place of death, burning and refuse). Tartarus, the prison of the Titans in Greek myth, appears once, in 2 Peter, which the book notes was among the most disputed books for the canon. The English word comes from an old Germanic word meaning to cover or conceal. By contrast, the Sermon on the Mount mentions the Kingdom of Heaven many times, and the book counts well over a hundred New Testament references to the Kingdom, most from Jesus. Of the 17 or so sermons in Acts, none focuses on the afterlife. The apostles preached a new King, his government, repentance and self-giving love. The emphasis on the afterlife grew mainly in the last five centuries. The book says the goal is to make our homes on earth more like heaven, rather than only to aim for heaven as our home.",
