    "category": "Words and translation",
    "myth": "Saying “OMG” or similar slang is what it means to take the Lord's name in vain.",
    "truth": "The Hebrew for “take” means to lift, carry or bear. To take God's name in vain is to carry his name falsely: claiming to be his while having no wish to live as his, or saying “God told me” when he did not. It is about how we represent him, not mainly about vocabulary.",
    "detail": "As a child the author had his mouth washed out with soap for saying “gosh” and “jeez”. He notes that “God” is a title, not God's personal name, which in Hebrew was written without vowels (YHWH) and was not pronounced. A Messianic rabbi explained the commandment to him with a marriage picture. When his wife took his name, doing so “in vain” would mean taking it and then running off with another man the next week. The commandment was given to Israel, not to Gentiles, and believers are not under that law. Still, if coarse speech becomes a stumbling block to others we should avoid it out of love (Romans 14:13).",
