    "myth": "The church has always used non-alcoholic grape juice for communion.",
    "truth": "Grape juice that would not ferment was not available until 1869. That year Thomas Welch, a Methodist, used pasteurisation to stop fermentation for his church during the temperance movement. For some 1,800 years the church used wine.",
    "detail": "Paul rebuked Corinthians who arrived early to the love feast and got drunk, which could not happen on grape juice. He later told the Ephesians not to be drunk with wine but filled with the Spirit. The author is not promoting alcohol and is clear that drunkenness is wrong. Everyone knows their own habits and limits, and he lives by Romans 14, not being a stumbling block over food, drink or dress. Churches can keep using juice. They should just say honestly that they do it out of Romans 14 love for those who struggle, not twist Scripture.",
