    "category": "Words and translation",
    "myth": "Acts 1:8 means the Spirit gives us power so we aren't afraid to knock on doors and tell neighbours about Jesus.",
    "truth": "The Greek <em>martus</em>, “witness”, is where we get “martyr”. Jesus was telling his apostles that the Spirit would empower them to bear witness to his death and resurrection and to be martyred themselves, as far as the ends of the earth.",
    "detail": "Every apostle except John was killed for his testimony, some as far away as India, as tradition says of Thomas. The book asks whether we really need supernatural power to tell a neighbour about the Saviour who changed our lives. Other religious groups, such as Jehovah's Witnesses and Mormons, knock doors more faithfully without claiming the Spirit's power. Sharing Christ is the natural overflow of the Godhead living in us. The power is for looking death in the eye: Peter reportedly asking to be crucified upside down, and Roman Christians said to have sung and forgiven as they died. Believers in places like Syria or Iraq today need that power just to admit their faith. The Spirit empowers us not only to live for Christ but to die for him if needed.",
    "scripture": "Acts 1:8; Acts 7:54-60; Revelation 2:13; John 21:18-19",
    "note": "In the New Testament <em>martus</em> mainly means “witness”. The specific sense of “martyr” developed in the following centuries, though Acts 22:20 and Revelation 2:13 show the link forming."
